.TH INVF-FSCK 8 "2026-09" "InvariantFS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
invf-fsck \- check and repair an InvariantFS volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B invf-fsck
.I image
.RB [ \-f | \-\-fix ]
.RB [ \-\-repair ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B invf-fsck
rebuilds the L2P table and the used-block bitmap from the inode area (AST
recipes are the source of truth for block ownership) and reports orphaned
blocks (allocated but referenced by no live AST), missing blocks
(referenced but free), stale L2P journal entries, and bad inode records
(CRC/length).
.PP
By default the tool is a read-only report.
.B \-f
applies the fixes: it rewrites the bitmap, the journal, and the superblock
state to CLEAN.
.PP
A volume that was interrupted (superblock DIRTY) normally needs no manual
fsck: at the next mount an anomaly-free scan self-heals it to CLEAN+rw.
.B INVFS_AUTO_RECOVER=0
disables that behaviour; this tool is the manual path.
.PP
On two-device (WP25) volumes a degraded mount (dev0 absent) is read-only:
the report mode works from the dev1 mirror, but
.B \-f
is refused until dev0 is reattached.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f , " \-\-fix"
Apply repairs instead of only reporting.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
WP20b layer-2 RS recovery: after the structural scan, reconstruct
CRC-failed shadow segments from the RS(32+m2, 32) parity. Stripes damaged
beyond m2 are reported and left untouched.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: suppress per-item progress.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when no issues were found (or all fixed); 1 on unrepairable errors or
open failure; 2 on usage errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR invf-verify (8),
.BR invf-sweep (8),
.BR invarifs (7)
